+### Compound Time Format
+
+Pass `compound: true` to break a duration into several units, largest first.
+
+```ts
+ms(5400000, { compound: true })       // "1h 30m"
+ms(90061000, { compound: true })      // "1d 1h 1m 1s"
+ms(3601000, { compound: true })       // "1h 1s"
+ms(-5400000, { compound: true })      // "-1h 30m"
+```
+
+- Units range from days (`d`) down to seconds (`s`); weeks, months and years are not used.
+- Zero-valued parts are skipped and parts are separated by a space.
+- Leftover milliseconds are dropped, so `500` formats as `"0s"`.
+- Negative values get a leading `-`.
+- `compound` takes precedence over `long`.
+- The output is for display: `ms()` cannot parse a multi-unit string like `"1h 30m"` back into a number.
+
